  "status": "published",
  "title": "Democrats Have at Least 258 House Seats. What Can They Do With That Majority?",
  "deck": "Democrats will control both chambers in January and plan to move first on health care costs. Their House majority gives them room to pass bills and investigate the administration, while the Senate’s rules and Trump’s veto power will shape what becomes law.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "Politics",
  "type": "news",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T12:23:13Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-23T12:23:13Z",
  "body": [
    "A North Carolina race called Sunday gave Democrats their 258th seat in the next House of Representatives. Now comes a harder set of numbers. The party expects to hold 56 seats in the Senate, where ending debate on most legislation takes 60 votes if opponents filibuster. And President Donald Trump can veto bills that reach his desk.",
    "Democrat Chuck Hubbard defeated Republican Rep. Virginia Foxx, bringing the party to at least 258 seats in the 435-member House. Four races remained uncalled when the Associated Press reported the result. It is the largest House majority either party has won since Democrats took 258 seats in 1992.",
    "What changes in the House",
    "The wide margin gives Democratic leaders room to bring bills to the floor even when some of their own members disagree. If all 435 members vote, a bill that needs a simple majority can pass with 218 votes. With 258 seats and the entire House voting, Democrats could lose 40 of their own and still pass such a bill without Republican votes.",
    "Control of the House also changes who runs committees and sets their agenda. Democratic chairs can schedule hearings and seek agency records. Committees can issue subpoenas under applicable House and committee rules. Those powers give the new majority a way to investigate federal spending and how the administration carries out existing laws without asking Trump to sign a bill.",
    "The first test could be health care costs",
    "The Associated Press reports that Democrats plan to move in their first 100 days to reverse recent Medicaid cuts and restore tax credits that help people buy coverage through the Affordable Care Act. That gives voters two specific proposals to watch when Congress takes office in January.",
    "The details will matter once bill text is available. Which Medicaid provisions would Congress reverse? Who would qualify for the restored tax credits, and when would the changes take effect? Those answers will show how much immediate help a proposal would offer households.",
    "Where the votes get harder",
    "With 56 Senate seats, Democrats would need four more votes to reach the usual 60-vote threshold for ending debate on most legislation if a filibuster is mounted and all 56 support cloture. Some measures can move under different rules, including qualifying budget legislation under reconciliation.",
    "A veto raises the bar again. Overriding Trump would take a two-thirds vote in each chamber among members voting, with a quorum present. With all 435 House members voting, that means 290 votes, or 32 more than the 258 Democratic seats called so far. The Senate would have to clear its own two-thirds threshold.",
    "The early test of the majority is whether its health care proposals draw support beyond the House. A floor vote will show what Democrats agree on. Senate votes and the White House’s response will show how much of the agenda has a path to becoming law."
  ],
